  "task1a_rounding_convention": {
    "white_table2_column_header": "Optimum lower bound",
    "white_quote_table_meaning": "In Table 2 we display the value of the objective function of the dual program for a verified feasible point in the dual space for several choices of input.",
    "white_quote_divide_conquer": "the optimum ends up close to 0.25 for any choice of N,T,R. In order to use our program to give a good bound on mu, we will need a divide and conquer strategy of breaking up the valid ranges ... into small chunks. The minimum of all the optimums over these smaller intervals will be our lower bound on mu.",
    "white_quote_table2_method": "Note that we have used the divide and conquer strategy mentioned above to narrow down the location of E(M*),c1* and d1*.",
    "white_quote_combination": "Combining all data from Table 2 shows that either mu>=0.37925 or [region (5.16)].",
    "finding": "Table-2 entries are by-construction VALID lower bounds (dual objective of a verified feasible point). BUT they are displayed to 2 dp (0.38) with NO explicit statement that they are rounded DOWN. White DID give 5 sig figs (0.37925) for the one entry near the binding threshold, evidence he shows more digits when a value is close to a decision boundary -- weak evidence the 0.38 entries are comfortably >0.380. White does NOT state 0.38 means >=0.380000; it could be round-to-nearest of a value in [0.375,0.385).",
